# CaR Management System — Feature & Functionality Specification

## 1. Purpose

A system for enterprises to manage their vehicle fleets: tracking which garages service which vehicles, running repairs through an approval workflow, billing against negotiated contracts, and rolling every cost up into a single per-vehicle expense ledger for reporting.

This document breaks the system into modules. Each module lists the functionality to build and its acceptance criteria, so it can be handed off as a self-contained set of tasks.

**Stack**: NestJS backend, Prisma ORM, CASL for authorization.

---

## 2. Modules

### 2.1 Enterprise & user management

**Functionality**
- Create, view, update, deactivate an enterprise (a company owning a fleet).
- Create, view, update, deactivate users within an enterprise.
- Roles are **dynamic**, not hardcoded: an admin can create a role (e.g. "Fleet Manager", "Approver", "Regional Auditor"), name it, and assign it one or more permissions.
- A permission is an (action, subject) pair in CASL terms — e.g. `approve` on `RepairRequest`, `create` on `Contract`, `manage` on `all`. One role can be assigned many permissions; a permission can belong to many roles.
- Each user is assigned exactly one role.
- **`Super_Admin`**: a reserved, system-created role.
  - It is never assigned any permissions in the database — its permission list stays empty.
  - It bypasses the permission system entirely: any authorization check for a `Super_Admin` user short-circuits to "allowed", rather than being evaluated against CASL rules. This must be implemented as an explicit check before CASL ability evaluation (e.g. `if (user.role.isSuperAdmin) return true;`), not by giving it a `manage all` permission — the requirement is that it has *no* assigned permissions and still bypasses.
  - It cannot be deleted, renamed, or edited via the normal role-management endpoints.
  - It is auto-created by the system, and automatically assigned to the very first user created (system bootstrap) — every subsequent user gets an explicitly assigned role.

**Acceptance criteria**
- A user always belongs to exactly one enterprise.
- Email is unique across the whole system (not just per enterprise).
- Deactivating a user does not delete their historical records (requests they made, approvals they gave).
- The first user ever created in the system is automatically assigned `Super_Admin`; this only happens once (guard against re-triggering on a second "first" user after data resets in a given environment).
- Attempting to assign a permission to `Super_Admin`, or to delete/rename it, is rejected by the API.
- Deleting a role that still has users assigned to it is blocked (or requires reassigning those users first — decide and document which).

